From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 14:14:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Dolby Vision: GUI composite hooks

Under native Dolby Vision the window composites Kodi's GUI itself: the
GUI is drawn into its own framebuffer and handed to the engine as an
sRGB layer, and an unchanged GUI can be reused instead of drawn again.
Two CWinSystemBase hooks, both false outside that window, let the rest
of Kodi take part:

- UsesNativeGuiCoverage(): GUI textures, fonts and quads blend for an
  sRGB layer with coverage alpha, not with the HDR composite's
  compensated alpha; PGS and other HDR subtitle overlays are
  drawn into the GUI layer with their palette converted to sRGB instead
  of being composited as HDR; the window manager does not trust the
  swapchain's buffer age for that separate framebuffer.
- CanReuseGuiComposite(): fullscreen video (and, with
  DV_PRIVATE_GUI_CACHE_HOME=1, the home screen) skips the GUI pass when
  nothing is dirty. The window manager counts every dirty mark
  (GetDirtyGeneration), so a mark made after the decision, by subtitle
  preparation for instance, still renders. The video controls no longer
  mark themselves dirty every frame there, as the video does not pass
  through the GUI.

OverlayRenderer also reports whether a buffer holds an HDR overlay
(HasHDROverlay) for the window. Kodi without the Dolby Vision window is
unchanged.

Formerly the GUI parts of yblod-03, with yblod-12 (GUI redraw), -13
(home screen cache) and -22 (PGS subtitles).
